Description: CVE-2026-14671 - refint: Remove plan cache
 Type confusion in contrib/refint allows an object creator to execute
 arbitrary code as the operating system user running the database.
 .
 refint kept prepared plans in a per-backend cache keyed by trigger name and
 relation OID. check_foreign_key() embeds the new key values literally in the
 SQL text of its cascade-UPDATE queries, so a cached plan re-executes the
 values captured at preparation time rather than the current ones. The cache
 is also never invalidated, so it can hand back stale entries whose plans no
 longer match the current relation, which is where the type confusion comes
 from.
 .
 Upstream chose not to repair the cache: refint is primarily sample code and
 there is a consensus to remove the module entirely in v20, so the plan cache
 is deleted outright and both check_primary_key() and check_foreign_key() now
 re-prepare on every call. The EPlan struct, the FPlans/PPlans arrays and
 find_plan() are removed along with the TopMemoryContext allocations and
 SPI_keepplan() calls that kept the plans alive across calls; the prepared
 plan is now an ordinary palloc'd local freed with the surrounding memory
 context.
 .
 The trigger regression test has to change as well: with the plan cache gone
 the refint sample triggers in that test must be AFTER rather than BEFORE
 triggers, otherwise the cascade-UPDATE case no longer produces the output the
 test expects. Upstream took these test changes from commit 8cfbdf8f4d for the
 back branches, and they are included here.
 .
 Backport notes for postgresql-13 13.23-0+deb11u4: upstream scoped this fix
 "Backpatch-through: 14" only because PostgreSQL 13 is EOL, not because 13 is
 unaffected -- the plan cache is present and reachable in 13.23. This backport
 is taken from the REL_14_STABLE result rather than from master, because
 Debian's own quilt patch
 0028-refint-Fix-SQL-injection-and-buffer-overruns.patch has already rewritten
 both functions in this tree to build their queries with a StringInfo, and
 REL_14_STABLE contains that same rewrite. Apart from the plan cache the two
 files are otherwise identical, so no further reconciliation was needed.
 .
 One upstream difference is deliberately excluded. REL_14_STABLE renders the
 cascade-UPDATE value as `nv ? quote_literal_cstr(nv) : "NULL"`, guarding
 against a NULL from SPI_getvalue(); that guard predates this fix and is not
 part of it, and Debian's 0028 backport does not have it. It is left out here
 to keep this patch scoped to the CVE. Note that this means the pre-existing
 NULL-dereference hazard on that line is untouched and still deserves a
 separate look.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/postgres/postgres/commit/66a5146dc73d3ac1befe6ab595815157d5281cb5
