Description: CVE-2026-19385 - pg_dump: avoid assuming how long pg_proc.protrftypes can be
 The backend imposes no limit on the length of pg_proc.protrftypes, and since
 it can contain entries for both input and output arguments the length may
 exceed FUNC_MAX_ARGS. pg_dump's dumpFunc() allocated exactly
 FUNC_MAX_ARGS * sizeof(Oid) for the parsed OID array and then filled it via
 parseOidArray(), so a function with more transform types than FUNC_MAX_ARGS
 caused a heap buffer overflow (CWE-122), leading to crashes or worse. In
 addition, if exactly FUNC_MAX_ARGS OIDs were present parseOidArray() did not
 zero-fill any trailing entry, letting the subsequent scan run off the end of
 the array and emit garbage.
 .
 This backport of upstream commit c2b16f5d495d977d669c7ec5db07bb666f3691e7
 redesigns parseOidArray() so that it allocates its own result array, and
 splits it into parseOidArray() (OIDs) and parseIntArray() (possibly-signed
 ints). When arraysize < 0 the array is dynamically sized to one more entry
 than the input and zero-terminated; when arraysize >= 0 the exact count is
 enforced. dumpFunc() now calls parseOidArray(protrftypes, -1) with no
 hard-coded bound, and getAggregates()/getFuncs()/getIndexes() are updated to
 the new self-allocating API. IndxInfo.indkeys becomes int * (via
 parseIntArray), removing the misleading Oid * type-punning. The PG13
 error-reporting idiom (pg_log_error() + exit_nicely(1)) is preserved instead
 of upstream's pg_fatal(). A regression test that creates a function with a
 transform is added to exercise the previously-untested dumpFunc() path.
Origin: backport, https://git.postgresql.org/gitweb/?p=postgresql.git;a=commit;h=c2b16f5d495d977d669c7ec5db07bb666f3691e7
Author: Tom Lane <tgl@sss.pgh.pa.us>
Last-Update: 2026-08-14
